.TH DBX-RESTORES 1 "2026-09-05" "dbx 0.41.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dbx-restores \- list and prune databases restored into the local containers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dbx restores
.RB [ list | prune ]
.RB [ \-\-older\-than
.IR D ]
.RB [ \-y ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every
.B dbx restore
leaves a database behind in
.B postgres-dbx
or
.BR mysql-dbx ,
and they add up.
.B dbx restores
joins the databases in those containers against the restore records in the
audit log
.RB ( ~/.local/share/dbx/audit.log )
and shows which ones a newer restore has superseded.
.PP
Each database gets a status:
.TP
.B keep
The newest restore of its source host/database, or younger than
.I D
days.
.TP
.B prune
Older than
.I D
days, and a newer restore of the same source exists.
.TP
.B untracked
No restore record: created by tests, worktrees or by hand. Listed, never
pruned.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B list
(default) Show every database with its status, size, age and source.
.TP
.B prune " [" \-y ]
Drop the databases marked
.BR prune .
Prompts for confirmation unless
.B \-y
(or
.BR \-\-yes )
is given; without a terminal it refuses to drop anything unless
.B \-y
is given.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-older\-than " D"
Age threshold in days (default 30).
.SH NOTES
Age counts from the restore, not from last use: Postgres and MySQL do not
record when a database was last queried.
.PP
After each successful restore, dbx prints a one-line warning when superseded
restores older than 30 days exist.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dbx (1),
.BR dbx-restore (1),
.BR dbx-containers (1)
